{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And prepare for me savory food</b> etc. <b>in order that my soul may bless you.</b> Isaac's intention in requesting the savory food was not for bodily pleasure and the sense of taste, but so that his soul would be joyful and delighted, for when the powers of the body are strengthened, the powers of the soul are roused, and from the soul's joy the holy spirit rests upon him. This is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: the Shekhinah does not rest neither from laziness nor from sadness, but from joy, as it is said (II Kings 3:15): ״והיה כנגן המנגן ותהי עליו יד ה׳״. And for this reason he mentions the soul each time together with the blessing, saying: בעבור תברכך נפשי — 'in order that my soul may bless you'; בעבור תברכני נפשך — 'in order that your soul may bless me'; למען תברכך נפשי — 'in order that my soul may bless you.' And the reason he requested savory foods for the joy of the soul and did not request a lyre to play, as was the custom of the prophets, is that he was about to bless him with physical things — ״דל השמים ומשמני הארץ״, ״ורב דגן ותירוש״ — and therefore he wanted the cause of the joy to be food of the same kind as that with which he wished to bless him." "<b>And behold</b> — this is similar to what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: why did the Torah say ״נסכו לפני מים בחג״? So that the rains of the year be blessed for you. Bring the omer before Him on Passover so that the grain in the fields be blessed for you; bring the two loaves before Him on Shavuot so that the fruit of the tree be blessed for you. And it is all measure for measure, that the blessing falls upon that same species through the merit of the bringing and the libation. And the intention of the Rabbis, of blessed memory, in this teaching is not that this should be the reward for performing these commandments, heaven forbid, for they said: ״שכר מצוה בהאי עלמא ליכא״ — there is no reward for a mitzvah in this world, that is to say, the principal reward is not in this world; rather, the reward consists of the fruits of the mitzvah that a person enjoys in this world in the very species of the mitzvah itself, while the principal remains for the World to Come. As the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: one who is careful with tzitzit merits a fine tallit; one who is careful with mezuzah merits a fine dwelling; one who is careful with the sanctity of the day merits and fills flasks of wine, and similar cases — the principal reward is not paid out in this world, but only the fruits of the mitzvah in its own kind."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:36.577224571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}